<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Education and Learning</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Trimester, I Am Learning About...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze the structure of various texts. Reading: Develop arguments based on one or more texts studied. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by practicing minimal pair sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze the structure of short educational texts (e.g., "Read about a subject and identify the introduction, body, and conclusion."). Can develop arguments based on reading about educational themes (e.g., "What subject would you study more and why?"). Can produce and differentiate between minimal pair sounds, accurately identifying and pronouncing distinct phonemes (e.g., /ɪ/ in "skill" versus /iː/ in "skull," /æ/ in "had" versus /e/ "head").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images representing different school subjects (e.g., a book for History, a globe for Geography, a calculator for Math, a laptop for Computer Science) and a picture of a student actively studying. Students are asked: "What subjects are you *learning* this *trimester*?" and "What *skills* are you *developing* in these subjects?" Students share their answers with a partner, recalling vocabulary from Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the previous lesson (e.g., *knowledge*, *career goals*, *academic*) and the target grammar. Teacher says: "This *trimester*, I *am learning* about new teaching *skills* to help you *grow*. I *would like* to improve my *knowledge* of technology." The teacher emphasizes the stress patterns in "I *am learning*" and "I *would like*."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the warm-up and modeled language.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'trimester' a week, a month, or a period of time like three months?" (A period of time like three months)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we use 'I *am learning*' for something happening now, or something that happened last year?" (Something happening now)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ (5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Education and Learning."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides students with a short educational text titled "My Trimester: Learning and Growing." This text describes a student's current subjects, *career goals*, and *skills* they are *developing*. It explicitly uses present continuous, gerunds after prepositions, and modals of desire. It also has clear introduction, body, and conclusion paragraphs. (Genre: Personal narrative/Informative blog post; Register: Semi-formal; Format: Short article).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will read to analyze the structure of the text (introduction, body, conclusion) and extract key information about learning goals and progress.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text quickly to answer: "What is the main idea of this article?" (Students are *actively learning* and setting *goals* this *trimester*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read more carefully to decide if statements about the text are true or false (e.g., "The student is *learning* *History* this *trimester*." "The student *is interested in learning* new *skills*." "The student *would like* to achieve their *career goals*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Structure Identification:</b> Students identify and label the introduction, body, and conclusion paragraphs in the text. Teacher asks: "Which paragraph tells us what the student *is learning* about generally? Which paragraphs describe specific *subjects* and *skills*? Which paragraph talks about future *career goals*?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ (10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying grammar structures (present continuous, gerunds after prepositions, modals for desire), target vocabulary (education, skills, knowledge, subject, grow, achieve, motivated, actively), and phonemic awareness (minimal pairs /ɪ/ vs /iː/, /æ/ vs /e/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Grammar & Vocab Cloze):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the text, but with blanks for target vocabulary (e.g., "I *am actively* _______ (learn) about new programming _______ (skill).") and grammar structures (e.g., "She *is interested in* _______ (develop) her *knowledge*."). Students fill in the blanks using the correct form of the word or verb.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Phonemic Awareness through Reading):</b> The teacher highlights sentences from the text containing words with minimal pair sounds (e.g., "I *have* many *skills*." "I *am feeling* *well* about my progress."). Students individually read these sentences aloud, focusing on differentiating sounds like /ɪ/ in "*skill*" vs. /iː/ (if a similar word exists in text or provided by teacher like "*feel*"), or /æ/ in "*had*" vs. /e/ in "*head*". The teacher provides a list of minimal pair words relevant to the text if direct examples are limited (e.g., *skill*/ *steel*, *had*/ *head*). Students then circle words in the text that feature these specific sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Text Analysis & Pragmatic Practice):</b> In pairs, students discuss specific sections of the text. One student reads a paragraph, and the other identifies if it's the introduction, body, or conclusion, and then summarizes its main point. They also ask each other pragmatic questions based on the text: "How *is* the student *doing* in science?" or "What *would* you *like* to *study* next *trimester*?" (referring to their own goals and offering encouragement like "You *should keep practicing* to get better" if a text character were struggling).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ (15-18 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Education and Learning."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Argument & Discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher presents a scenario: "Imagine your school wants to add one new *educational subject* to the *schedule* next *trimester*. Based on the text we read and your own *career goals*, your group needs to agree on ONE new *subject* and *develop* an argument explaining why it's the best choice for the school. Make sure your argument has an introduction, body, and conclusion." Students are encouraged to use the target grammar (e.g., "We *are arguing* for Computer Science because students *are interested in learning* new digital *skills*." "I *would like* to see more students *grow* their *knowledge* in this area.") and vocabulary. They also engage in sociolinguistic discussions, respecting others' opinions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher's Role:</b> Facilitate group discussions, provide language support, monitor for the use of target language and discussion of personal/learning goals, and ensure all group members actively participate in developing their arguments.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ (10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their chosen *subject* and argument to the class (1-2 minutes per group), focusing on the structure (introduction, body, conclusion) they developed. As a follow-up, students complete an individual "Exit Ticket."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist for Group Presentation):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clearly identified introduction, body, and conclusion in their argument.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., *education*, *skills*, *knowledge*, *career goals*, *motivated*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used present continuous, gerunds after prepositions, or modals for desire correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Argument is logical and coherent.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated intelligible pronunciation, particularly of identified minimal pair sounds during presentation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engaged in respectful discussion (Sociolinguistic).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one *skill* you practiced today when analyzing the text's structure?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did you use new vocabulary to discuss *educational* *goals* in your group?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ (5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and connection to next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection (Oral or written prompts):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of analyzing text structure or *developing* an argument did I find most challenging today, and how can I *grow* in this *skill*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did practicing minimal pair sounds help me read *well* today?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students effectively analyze the structure of the provided text?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to *develop* coherent arguments using the target language and structure?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific reading *skills* need more attention in future lessons? Were the minimal pair practices *well* received and effective?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed to scaffold the speaking and interaction for the next lesson?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we *actively* read about *educational goals* and *developed* arguments. In our next lesson (Lesson 3), we will use this *knowledge* and our critical thinking *skills* to *grow* our speaking ability by sharing our *career goals* and discussing our progress with our *classmates*."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ (3-5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>